# OneSignal Safety Rules

When using OneSignal tools or generating OneSignal API code, treat sends and writes as customer-impacting operations.

## Secrets

- Never ask the user to paste API keys, OAuth tokens, private keys, or other secrets into chat or source files.
- Never commit `.env` files or real credentials.
- Redact keys in logs, examples, screenshots, and support text.

## Read Before Write

Prefer read-only checks before writes:

- Use health/config/reference tools before workflow-specific tools.
- Inspect existing segments, templates, messages, users, or journeys before creating duplicates.
- Surface app id, audience, channel, and object ids before any mutation.

## Explicit Approval

Require clear user approval before:

- Sending or scheduling push, email, SMS, or in-app messages.
- Creating, updating, deleting, pausing, or activating segments, templates, journeys, users, subscriptions, aliases, live activities, or exports.
- Transferring subscriptions or making identity changes.
- Acting on production apps.

For message sends, the approval summary must include channel, app id, audience, estimated count when available, content, schedule/timezone, and whether this is a test send.

For large audiences or any production blast, ask for an extra confirmation after showing the estimated count.

## Safer Defaults

- Default to read-only investigation when intent is ambiguous.
- Prefer test users, test segments, and test apps before broad sends.
- Do not assume "everyone" means every user record; clarify subscribed audience and channel.
- Do not bypass Cursor MCP approval prompts.
- If a OneSignal capability is unavailable through MCP, explain the limitation and provide dashboard/API guidance instead of inventing a tool.
